    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493295880/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """[ Two ways of describing an afternoon tea]
We all lunched in town. Bernhard at the Scotts, Janet and I with the Herricks (Mrs. Herrick is charming) and the others at Gilli e Letta’s. Lion and Bobby stayed to dinner. The doctor came and pronounced me much better. 
Thursday, Apr. 16, 1896, Fiesole
Bernhard showed Lion and Bobby some photos in the morning, and I worked on Pliny proofs with Miss Sellers. Maud Cruttwell came to lunch, and dear Carlo Placci afterwards, who played “Tristan” to us Bernhard and Bertie came in to tea from a long climb with Mr. Benn.
Miss Sellers was most amusing about how Anatole France, or any snobbish writer might describe the scene.  “Madame Costelloe, étendue sur sa chaise longue avec de fourreurs, etc., des groupes des jeunes gens se détachaient, discutant la politique italienne. Un jeune homme de la haute noblesse, que avait laissait d’Ambassade anglaise à Paris pour se """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 144 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1895-96> .

